WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:11.290
CHRISTINE LO: Well,
good morning, everyone.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.680
I'm really excited to be
here today to share with you

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.800
how we've used Google
Cloud Platform to analyze

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.030
genomic data.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.100
So, a little bit about
myself, I'm Christine,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.250
and I lead a team of software
engineers and bioinformaticians

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.810
at Myriad.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.270
As a hybrid team, we're not
just interested in analyzing

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.750
genomic data, but we're
also interested in choosing

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.670
the right tools to
do so, especially

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.360
in a clinical lab
setting of our size.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.930
So, first of all, who are we?

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.830
We are a genetic testing
lab with a mission

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:44.990
to deliver genomic
information to patients

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.700
at each stage of their life.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.280
So this requires a deep
understanding of technology

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.870
across many different domains.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.290
So, for example, we need to
have a deep understanding

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.108
of genomic data
to understand how

00:00:57.108 --> 00:00:59.150
to choose the right tools
to gather and structure

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.980
genomic data,
interpret it, and then

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.730
ultimately deliver the right
information to patients.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.160
So, that's from a
mission standpoint.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.770
From a product standpoint, we
have three different products

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.650
for delivering genomic
information to patients

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.400
at different stages of life.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.150
So, our first test is our
carrier screening test,

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.820
this is for couples who are
planning on getting pregnant

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.800
and want to know if
they are both carriers

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.360
for a recessive disease.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.180
So we can screen for over
200 inherited recessive

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.490
conditions including
cystic fibrosis,

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.760
spinal muscular
atrophy, and Fragile X.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.690
Our prenatal test is for
women who are pregnant.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.220
And this test detects fetal
chromosomal abnormalities

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.500
as early as 10 weeks
into the pregnancy.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.030
This replaces insensitive
ultrasounds and also invasive

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.930
amniocentesis.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.440
And finally, our inherited
cancer screening test

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:52.950
is for men and women.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.380
And it tests for BRCA1, BRCA2
related breast and ovarian

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.450
cancers, as well as
several other types

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:00.465
of inherited cancers.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.080
So, to get a sense
of scale, this

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.360
is a broad market
with growing demand.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.750
There are currently 4.2 million
pregnant women in the United

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.760
States annually, and
we've really only reached

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.680
the tip of the iceberg
in terms of delivering

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.210
genetic information to them.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.760
With 1/5 of them receiving
carrier screening tests,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.880
and 1/3 of them receiving
non-invasive prenatal testing.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.590
The market for inherited
cancer screening

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.470
is even larger with more
opportunities for growth.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.740
With 19 million women who meet
criteria for inherited cancer

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.350
screening, and only
1.5 of them actually

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.230
receiving preventative testing.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.160
So, how do we do this?

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.530
We're a clinical lab.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:46.470
And how do we actually
test these patients

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.430
for these different diseases?

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.580
So, it turns out the
typical flow of a sample

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.180
is actually quite similar
across all of our products.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.460
We essentially receive a
tube of blood or saliva

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.630
from the patient and then we
sequence the sample in our lab.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.650
Depending on those
product we will

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.500
do either high coverage
targeted sequencing,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.720
or low coverage whole
genome sequencing.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.480
After sequencing is
complete, we align the reads

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.730
to the reference genome
and look for variants

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.830
in that sample's genome.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.780
After going through
QC, it then moves

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.810
into the variant
interpretation phase.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.580
Variant interpretation
is the step

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.620
that is to determine
which variants

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.955
are disease causing or not.

00:03:27.955 --> 00:03:30.400
Not all variants are
actually disease causing,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.460
and so we need to find
the ones that are,

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.340
and actually calculate
risk as well in this step.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.112
So, finally, once we have all
of the interpreted information

00:03:40.112 --> 00:03:42.070
we are ready to send the
report to the patient.

00:03:44.702 --> 00:03:46.160
So in this talk,
I'm going to focus

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.180
on how we use GCP
for the problem

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.860
of variant interpretation.

00:03:56.047 --> 00:03:58.130
So, first, let's talk about
some of the challenges

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.740
of variant interpretation.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.990
The challenges of
variant interpretation

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:03.460
come in two different flavors.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.830
One is the scalability
flavor, and then the other

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.600
is the diverse
set of information

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.890
that we need to
interpret variants.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.470
So, to really
understand the challenge

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.060
of variant
interpretation, we first

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.140
need to understand the
scale of the problem.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.320
So how much data
are we dealing with?

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.070
So, as Myriad, we are the
largest molecular diagnostics

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:28.080
company, and we have tested over
two million patients to date.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.720
All of our tests
are focused which

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.540
means that we test specific
genes and diseases that we have

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.700
predetermined are actionable.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.610
Each product we test a different
set of genes and diseases.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:45.260
But in total, we test for
over 200 genes and diseases.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:49.023
So, what does this mean in
terms of base pairs sequenced?

00:04:49.023 --> 00:04:50.440
Again, this depends
on our product

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.650
but we can think of, on
average, sequencing around one

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.080
million base pairs per person.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.480
Some of our tests do low
coverage full genome sequencing

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.900
and some of our other tests have
a smaller region of interest,

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.720
but on average we will sequence
around one million base pairs

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:05.900
per person.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.870
So, on the database level,
what does this mean?

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:11.350
We need to keep track of
all of the region sequenced

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.190
in a person.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.950
So, to blow that
into perspective,

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.040
we want to keep track of
the one million times two

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.780
million base pairs that we've
ever sequenced in the lab.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.530
We also want to keep
track of the variants

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.950
that we see per person.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.820
So, most of the time, the
genomic sequence of a person

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.520
is the same as the
average person.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.500
Variability in
humans are estimated

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.060
to be 1 in 1,000 base pairs.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.420
So, a naive database
representation

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.510
of the number of variants
that we see per person

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:43.190
would be a million
divided by 1,000,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.580
which is 1,000 variants
per person, multiplied

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.640
by the 2 million patients that
we have tested, or 2 billion

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:50.140
rows.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.840
So, that gives you an idea of
the amount of sequencing data

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.270
that we need to keep track of.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.150
For variant
interpretation, we want

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.980
to know what the number
of novel variants

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.830
are because we want to
determine for each variant

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.410
whether or not it's
disease causing or not.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.110
So this graph just shows
that even though you've

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.350
been testing for a while, there
is a long tail of rare variants

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.750
that exist.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:19.627
A vast majority of these
variants are benign.

00:06:19.627 --> 00:06:21.710
But a small percentage
actually break the function

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.200
that they are in,
and the problem

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.260
of variant interpretation is
to determine which one of these

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.770
are disease causing or not.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.410
So this gives you an idea
of the scale of the problem,

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.960
but we need to understand
the other challenge, which

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.490
is the many different
factors that

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.070
are used to determine the
classification of a variant.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:41.540
So for each novel
variant we need

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.040
to determine if
it's disease causing

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.490
or not by gathering and weighing
different types of evidence.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.820
So, you don't actually
have to read this,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.550
but these are guidelines that
are produced by the American

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.100
Society of Medical
Genetics that just show

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.770
the multiple types of
evidence that one needs

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:03.130
to consider when determining
the classification of a variant.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:06.020
I'm going to just highlight
one of these types of evidence,

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.265
which is a allele frequency.

00:07:07.265 --> 00:07:08.890
Knowing the allele
frequency can really

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.350
help determine the
pathogenicity of the variant.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.920
Because if we see
this allele occur

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.120
more often than the
disease itself occurs,

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.740
then it is less likely to
be a pathogenic variant.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.350
And so you can classify
this allele as benign if it

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.060
has a high allele frequency.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.080
So what is allele frequency?

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.770
It's pretty straightforward.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.580
It's calculated as a
fraction, the number of times

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.490
that we see an allele in a
population over the number

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:39.790
of times that this particular
locus of the allele

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.650
was sequenced in the population.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.520
This is fairly
straightforward but just want

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.950
to highlight that we need to
parse a lot of genomic data

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.190
to get this calculation.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:53.830
For AC, which is
allele count, we

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.740
need to go through all of
the calls of every sample

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.120
to calculate this.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:01.570
And for AN, which is
the number of times

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:03.940
that we've sequenced
a particular locus,

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.550
we need to go through all
of the region sequenced

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.387
in every single sample.

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:09.720
So that's a lot of genomic data.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:15.290
Another type of evidence
is zygosity count.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:19.180
So, zygosity count is similar
to allele frequency in that it's

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.480
counting the number of
times a certain genotype is

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.170
seen in the population.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.560
So since humans are
diploids, we have two copies

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:27.910
of each of our chromosome.

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.670
One is from our mom, and
one is from our father.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:33.130
So most people are the same
as the reference genome,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.710
and so that makes them
homozygous for the reference

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:36.730
sequence.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.580
Some people have a mutation on
one copy of their chromosome

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.500
making them heterozygous.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:44.020
And sometimes a
person has a mutation

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:45.850
on both copies of
their chromosome,

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.210
making them homozygous for
the alternate sequence.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:53.050
And in some rare cases,
a person actually

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:54.550
only has one copy
of the chromosome

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:58.120
at a particular locus, making
them what we call a hemizygote.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.770
So for variant
interpretation, we're

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.780
really interested in
knowing the zygosity count

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:05.710
because if several
people are homozygous

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:10.210
for the alternate sequence,
then that makes this allele less

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:11.560
likely to be disease causing.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:16.760
All of these
questions really fall

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:21.770
under a class of analyses
called tertiary analysis, where

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.060
we try to make sense
of sequencing data.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.450
So just to contrast between
primary analysis, which

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.690
is really trying to interpret
the data that's coming off

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.060
of the sequencing machines
and output sequencing

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.450
reads, and contrasted with
secondary analysis, which

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:41.060
aims to assemble and align
the reads to a genome and call

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:41.930
variants.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:45.500
Tertiary analysis tries to
make sense of this variation

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.792
in a biological way.

00:09:47.792 --> 00:09:49.250
So other types of
questions that we

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.610
may want to ask when we're
doing tertiary analysis

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:56.390
would be how often do we
see a combination of alleles

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:59.570
together in one patient?

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:02.920
We also might want to
calculate these metrics

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.940
on different segments
of the population.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.960
So we may want to segment
by ethnicity, for example.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.910
Sometimes an allele can
have a high frequency

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.867
in one ethnicity,
but not others,

00:10:11.867 --> 00:10:13.450
and that would give
it enough evidence

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:15.868
to classify it as benign.

00:10:15.868 --> 00:10:17.410
We may also want to
segment by gender

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.868
because there are some diseases,
like cystic fibrosis, that

00:10:19.868 --> 00:10:21.520
have different
outcomes for gender,

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:25.630
and so you might want to look
only at the female population

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:27.610
if you want to assume
a healthy population.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.450
Again, we always, when
calculating these metrics,

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:37.140
we do want to assume that
we have a healthy population

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.810
so we potentially
want to sequence out

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.300
any patients with any
sort of testing indication

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.850
that's not routine
carrier screening.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:46.500
That means that they
might be getting

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:51.210
tested for a particular reason,
and they are probably actually

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:55.320
not considered to be part
of the healthy population.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.790
Furthermore, for
research purposes,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.620
we really want to make sure that
we are only including samples

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:02.160
that have consented to
research, and are in research

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.550
allowed jurisdictions.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:06.240
So this is another type of
segmentation of population

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.900
that we are interested
in the research realm.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:12.850
Finally, tertiary
analysis does not

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.250
need to be limited to
variant interpretation.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.280
We may, for example, want to
infer ethnicity of patients

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.660
based on their
genetic information.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:25.950
And this falls under the
realm of tertiary analysis.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.470
So for a lot of
these questions we

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.278
can utilize public databases.

00:11:29.278 --> 00:11:31.070
We can see if the
variant has been observed

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:32.960
in that particular
population, and look

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:35.060
at what the allele
frequency, and the zygosity

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.310
are in that population.

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:41.210
But some caveats are that these
populations databases are often

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:45.080
limited in size, which means
that rare variants are less

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:47.810
likely to be observed,
and if they are observed,

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:52.020
they may have a good chance
of being overrepresented.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.870
Furthermore, we aren't actually
able to answer some questions

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.940
like co-occurrence without
knowing the genotype

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:00.390
at an individual level.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.090
So, we do use these
public data sets

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:06.500
but these are just one type
of evidence that we consider.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:08.690
Really, in order to provide
the best information

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.600
to our patients, we have to
leverage our own internal data

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.650
set of over 2 million patients.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.720
With this larger
population, we have

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:20.210
much more statistical power
for variant interpretation.

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:24.190
And we can also go beyond
variant interpretation

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:28.510
and contribute back to science,
and do our own retrospective

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:29.540
analyses

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.110
So, again, when
we're doing research

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.017
we have to be very
careful in making sure

00:12:34.017 --> 00:12:36.100
that our samples have been
consented for research,

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.250
and are in research
allowed jurisdictions.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:42.260
But once we have
that population,

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.010
then we can, for
example, ask questions

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.730
like what is our carrier
rate to understand

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:51.730
more of the efficacy of expanded
carrier screening, especially

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:57.560
in a diverse ethnic population.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:58.900
So, what is the carrier rate?

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.233
What is the at risk couple rate?

00:13:00.233 --> 00:13:03.370
Those are all very interesting
questions to basically prove

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.880
efficacy of our tests.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:08.710
With this platform
we can also observe

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:12.430
other patterns, such as coupling
patterns across ethnicities.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:18.205
And we can observe many
other types of these analysis

00:13:18.205 --> 00:13:19.330
with the data that we have.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.320
So, really, hopefully
this motivates

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.060
building a system
for tertiary analysis

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:32.370
that requires tackling
two major challenges.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:37.050
The first one is scalability,
and the second is flexibility.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.960
We need a flexible system
because unlike clinical trials

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.330
or other hypotheses
driven projects,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.520
the set have metadata
that might be relevant

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:48.450
for analysis is diverse and
potentially unknown upfront.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.250
So, for example,
I could ask to see

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:52.020
all of the homozygous
individuals that

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.470
have a co-occurring carrying
pathogenic mutation.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.630
One group may want to see
a certain set of metadata

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.360
for variant interpretation, and
another group may want to see

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.170
another set of metadata for
retrospective . analysis.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.960
Furthermore, different groups
may have different restrictions

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.900
and access to data.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:10.820
So we really need
a solution that

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:14.570
allows for flexible ad
hoc aggregation, and cyber

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:17.450
filtering of
variant information,

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:20.120
along with sample metadata.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.970
We also need a scalable system.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:25.490
We have a very large
population data set,

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:28.410
and this population data set
is growing every single day.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:33.360
So unlike some of the public
databases, or academic cohorts,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.750
this is a patient
population that

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.550
is growing every
single day and we

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:42.230
need to calculate these metrics
on the latest information.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:52.430
So, I just wanted to talk
about one implementation

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.170
that we had before
using BigQuery.

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:57.320
So, we essentially hand built
a batch compute with Luigi

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.490
to calculate allele frequencies.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:05.150
This pipeline was very
optimized for the one metric

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:07.730
that it needed to calculate.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:09.830
We basically took
advantage of the fact

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.800
that this was a
recursive procedure,

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:18.470
and stored the
previous day's ACANAF

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:22.190
and would add to that
as needed as we got

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:25.490
new samples every single day.

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:29.390
But some of the problems
that we ran into

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:31.830
was that it was not flexible.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:34.430
So, if we wanted to
add another metric

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.280
we had to build a completely
different pipeline.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:41.180
And these would often
take weeks to run

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.650
on all of our historical data.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:46.970
We could obviously
increase the instant size

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:48.890
and the compute power,
but it would still

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:52.370
be very compute intensive.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:59.120
The other thing is
that it's really not

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.720
able to do ad hoc
aggregation and filtering.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:04.820
You really have to be
thinking about what

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.970
is the information that
you want upfront, and then

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:10.220
and then building the
pipeline to calculate it.

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:13.970
So it's not a very
flexible system.

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:19.400
It's not scalable in the sense
that we aren't able to really

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:23.270
generate these metrics in an ad
hoc way with the amount of data

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.760
that we have in the
time that scientists

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:29.340
need to see them in real time.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.460
So that kind of
motivates why we decided

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:34.460
to look into using BigQuery.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:37.790
So this is just a very high
level architectural overview

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:38.930
of our system.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:43.370
We have genomic
data and sample data

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:48.020
that is stored on
Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:50.960
This is ingested into
BigQuery using Dataflow,

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.710
and then right now, we have
just a very simple system

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:58.830
that persists the results
back into Cloud Storage.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:03.330
So as new data comes in, we
add new rows to BigQuery.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:06.670
So let me just go into each
of the individual steps.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:10.480
So, this is the input data
that we're dealing with.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.575
We store genomic
data and sample data.

00:17:14.575 --> 00:17:16.200
I will go into a
little bit more detail

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.000
about what the genomic
data looks like.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.130
The sample data is
anything that you

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:21.630
might want to know
about the sample,

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.660
so that would be gender,
ethnicity, the testing

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:29.520
indications, family
history, et cetera.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.570
And for the genomic information,
we store this in a format

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:35.620
called GVCF

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.040
So, if you work
with genomic data,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.590
you may be familiar with
the concept of VCF which

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.700
is the variant call format.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:44.520
This is a file that
basically contains

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.785
all of the call information
for a particular sample.

00:17:47.785 --> 00:17:51.630
A genomic VCF is very
similar to the VCF,

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:54.640
but it also includes the
homozygous reference regions.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.940
So, essentially, this
allows us to distinguish

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.710
between regions
that were sequenced,

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.000
and regions that were not.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:02.550
And, so you can
see that there are

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:04.800
large blocks of
homozygous reference

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:07.800
regions in this particular
sample that indicates

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:09.390
that this person
was sequenced here,

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:10.950
but no variants
were called here.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:18.460
So, we have all of
this information,

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.650
genomic information
and sample information,

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:23.128
in our Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:23.128 --> 00:18:25.420
The next thing we want to do
is transform and load this

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:26.470
into BigQuery.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:28.420
So, the way that we
chose to do this was we

00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:31.300
chose to parse the GVCFs
into, and transform them

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:33.400
into a parquet format.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:36.250
With this, we're able to really
be flexible about the schema

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.590
that we ultimately
want in BigQuery.

00:18:43.323 --> 00:18:44.740
And then at the
end of the day, we

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.990
have these parquet files
that we could potentially use

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:50.950
in other Cloud systems as well.

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:54.790
So, we parse our GVCFs,
every single sample,

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:58.270
transform them into parquet
and then append the new samples

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.170
to our BigQuery table.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:05.670
And similarly, we do this with
the sample amount of data.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:08.990
So this is an example of what
the BigQuery table ends up

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:13.520
looking like after we
load it in with Dataflow.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:15.860
As you can see, it's
very similar to the GVCF

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:18.510
that I showed earlier.

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:22.460
It has your chromosome
position, ref, alt

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:26.730
and the genotype
information available.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:32.720
So this is kind of a high level
overview of a sample query.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.650
So to calculate
allele account, we've

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:38.730
split this up into
two steps, really.

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:40.470
The first is to parse
the genotype field

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.440
so that every single
row represents

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:45.350
a single allele for a sample.

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:50.270
And then after we do this,
after we flatten the table,

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.760
we are able to aggregate
all the alleles together

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:55.320
to generate the allele count.

00:19:55.320 --> 00:20:03.180
And, so this is a simple groupi,
and then a count in aggregate.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:06.610
So, I wanted to
talk a little bit

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:09.110
about some of the tricky
cases that we have dealt with,

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:10.990
because on the
surface level, this

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:13.690
may seem like very
straightforward to do.

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:15.190
But there are some
tricky edge cases

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:16.280
that we have to deal with.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:19.640
And one of them is the
duplication of samples.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:22.630
So, there's a possibility
that patients can get tested

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:24.610
multiple times in our lab.

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:26.500
And so, as a
result, there can be

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.090
multiple samples
that are associated

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.250
with a particular patient.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:32.680
And we want to use
the most recent sample

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:36.180
because those are assumed
to be the most accurate.

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:38.020
In our previous
pipeline implementation,

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:40.353
we had to do this by
calculating them--

00:20:40.353 --> 00:20:42.520
since we were calculating
the metrics incrementally,

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.210
we had to basically subtract out
the counts of the samples that

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.460
were duplicate.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:56.470
And append that subtraction
to our final allele count.

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:59.860
But with BigQuery,
it's much easier

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:01.810
to deduplicate the
samples upfront,

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.295
and then recompute your metrics.

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:09.500
So, the other tricky case
that I wanted to talk about

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.615
is the deduplication
of alleles in BigQuery.

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:18.230
There can be multiple
representations

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:21.530
of the same allele So, when
working with genomic data,

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:25.130
we often define
alleles according

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:26.300
to the reference sequence.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:29.090
But, as you guys may know,
there are many versions

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:32.810
of the reference genome, and
even with the same reference

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:36.110
genome, there are potentially
multiple representations

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:37.830
in very repetitive regions.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:40.010
So this particular
example is an example

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:43.220
of an insertion deletion
and indel in a repetitive

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:44.200
region of the genome.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:45.950
And you can see that
there are potentially

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:49.430
four different
representations of this allele

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:51.570
when compared to the
reference genome.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.960
So this is a known problem
in the field of genomics.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:57.380
There are many working
groups out there.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:59.030
The Global Alliance
for Genomic Health

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:02.960
that are dedicated to
solving this problem.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:05.890
So, how we've decided
to address this problem

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:09.670
is very similar to the way that
Global Alliance for Genomic

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:11.860
Health has also
addressed it, by using

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:13.490
a content addressable hash.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.760
So, this is an
internally built library

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:19.870
that we built that is used
to tag alleles upfront.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:23.440
So then we'll tag
them with the hash,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:27.640
and then it makes it much
easier to combine alleles later

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:31.410
in the BigQuery pipeline.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:37.540
And so finally, the real
advantage of BigQuery

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:39.930
is this continuous
ingestion of data.

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:43.350
We're able to essentially just
add rows to the BigQuery table

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:46.020
as we see more samples
coming through our lab.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:50.530
And because it's so
easy to query on this,

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:56.620
we can just recompute our
metrics on the entire table.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:01.640
So, then how do we actually
add more genomic calculations

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.290
in BigQuery?

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:06.200
It's as simple as writing
another SQL query.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.500
So we can do this, and done this
for a co-occurrence, zygosity,

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:11.390
and dependent samples.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:14.300
Furthermore, putting
it in BigQuery

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:18.200
allows us to do a lot of ad hoc
filtering, and segmentation. we

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.720
can filter and
segment on ethnicity,

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:25.000
and gender, testing
indication, et cetera.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.370
So this really allows
us the scalability

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.290
that we were looking
for and the flexibility

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:33.560
that we were looking for in
terms of ad hoc filtering.

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:43.660
So, to wrap up, I wanted
to talk about application

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:45.830
in cystic fibrosis.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:49.510
Cystic fibrosis is one of the
most common autosomal recessive

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:52.330
conditions according to ACOG.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:55.330
It's caused by a mutation
in the CFTR gene, which

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:57.510
codes for the CFTR protein.

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:02.500
CFTR protein acts as a channel
on the membranes of the cells

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:04.900
to control for the flow of
water and ions in and out

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:07.790
of the cell,
especially in the lung.

00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:11.650
So, if there's a pathogenic
mutation on both chromosomes,

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.420
this will cause cystic fibrosis.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:20.440
It does this by basically
reducing the flow of these ions

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:22.660
and as a result,
there's a thick mucus

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:25.450
that tends to form on
the surface of the cells.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:27.695
So this causes lung infections
and limits the ability

00:24:27.695 --> 00:24:28.570
to breathe over time.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.590
So, as a carrier
screening lab, we

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:39.090
have the advantage of having a
very large healthy population.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:41.405
So with this very large
healthy population

00:24:41.405 --> 00:24:43.280
we are actually able to
use our internal data

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:45.590
to reclassify some
variants that we previously

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:49.160
did not have enough
evidence to classify.

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:51.710
And as a result, they
were considered variants

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:53.620
of unknown significance.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.640
Variants of unknown
significance are

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.520
just variants that we've
exhausted all the information

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.580
we know about this, but we
still don't know whether or not

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.160
it's disease causing or not.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:05.660
These types of variants makes
it really difficult to counsel

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:08.090
patients because
it's obviously better

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:13.568
to know definitively whether
it's benign or pathogenic.

00:25:13.568 --> 00:25:15.110
So this particular
variant, we wanted

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:17.450
to know how many
patients had this allele

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:20.600
along with another
pathogenic variant.

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:25.010
If this particular
allele occurs very often

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.920
with another pathogenic
variant, it's much less likely

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:30.200
that this is actually
a pathogenic variant

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:32.600
because the fact that we
have a healthy population

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:35.510
means that these people
are alive and well.

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:40.440
And they don't have
cystic fibrosis.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.465
So we asked that
question, and it turns out

00:25:42.465 --> 00:25:44.090
that there were quite
a few people that

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:48.140
had a co-occurring mutation
with a pathogenic mutation.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.670
But then we wanted to
go a little bit further.

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:54.020
So cystic fibrosis is
an interesting disease

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:57.330
in that there is a variable
phenotype for males.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:00.230
And so, it's very
likely that there

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:02.210
may be males with
cystic fibrosis

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:03.210
that don't even know it.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.570
And so to filter out
the males, and just

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:09.170
look at the number of
co-occurring pathogenic

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:11.090
mutations in a
female population,

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:14.510
that gives us a much
stronger statistical power

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:17.995
to classify this
variant as benign.

00:26:17.995 --> 00:26:19.620
And ultimately we
were able to do that.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:24.090
We were able to downgrade
this variant to benign.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.378
This is another example
where we actually did not

00:26:26.378 --> 00:26:27.920
have enough information
to downgrade,

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:29.337
so we asked the
question, how many

00:26:29.337 --> 00:26:34.010
patients has this has
this mutation co-occurring

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:35.962
with a pathogenic mutation?

00:26:35.962 --> 00:26:37.670
And the conclusion
was that there was not

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:41.070
enough to downgrade.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:44.280
And then finally, for
this particular variant,

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:45.960
we also were able
to reclassify it,

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:50.775
but we actually reclassified
it from pathogenic to VUS.

00:26:50.775 --> 00:26:52.650
Given the answers to
some of these questions,

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:54.516
we actually think
it's probably benign.

00:26:54.516 --> 00:26:57.180
But the fact that we're
not giving a false positive

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:02.260
to a patient is actually
is better than that.

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.140
So to downgrade this
variant, we actually

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:07.220
use both allele frequency,
and co-occurring statistics

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:09.180
to downgrade.

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:12.480
So it turns out that
this allele actually

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:15.990
occurs quite a lot in a
healthy patient population.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:20.790
So, it's considered to be a
very, very strong candidate

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:22.800
for actually being benign.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:27.050
